  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>In the bay of Canebiers (spelled "Canoubiers" by the tourist office), this family-friendly sandy beach is beautifully exposed to the sunset over the Maures mountains. Its name reportedly comes from the Provençal canebe (hemp), which has nothing to do with the reeds growing there today.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>As on all the town's beaches, municipal order no. 1828/2026 of 6 July 2026 allows dogs on the town's beaches from 6am to 9.30am and 6.30pm to 10pm, from 16 July to 15 September 2026; outside those hours they are not allowed. Dogs must stay under their owner's constant responsibility and be kept on a leash when approaching other users and whenever circumstances require; the order does not apply to walkers with dogs on a leash on the coastal path. It only covers that period: for the rest of the year, we found no published rule, so rely on the signage on site. This beach's listing on Saint-Tropez Tourisme's site, however, says "dogs are not allowed" with no mention of hours — probably a listing not updated since the order, but that isn't guaranteed. Check the signage on site before settling in with your dog.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: save this spot for late afternoon (the 6.30pm-10pm window) to enjoy one of the peninsula's best sunsets over the Maures with your dog.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>To the east come <a href="/en/fiche-plage-moutte-saint-tropez">La Moutte</a> and <a href="/en/fiche-plage-salins-saint-tropez">Les Salins</a>; towards the town centre, you will find <a href="/en/fiche-plage-graniers-saint-tropez">Graniers</a>. A sailing club is located near the beach (tourist office).</p>
    </div>

